# The vendored cores are content-addressed deliverables, not text.
#
# Every file under these folders is pinned by sha-256: `manifest.json` records
# the hash of each file beside it, `src/lib/heart.ts` pins CORE_WASM_SHA256, and
# the differential harness in Groloo-core refuses to evaluate a single fixture
# unless the bytes on disk hash to what the manifest claims.
#
# Git's autocrlf breaks all of that on Windows. The glue is stored correctly as
# LF in the repository, but a Windows checkout rewrites every line ending on the
# way out, and the file grows by one byte per line:
#
#     heart/0.1.0-5ecaa78/stredio_heart.js       31,733 in git -> 32,585 on disk
#     groloo-core/<build>/groloo_core.js         30,380 in git -> 31,222 on disk
#
# The hash then fails and the failure reads as "the vendored artifact is stale"
# or "corrupt", which is what it was mistaken for. Nothing was ever stale: strip
# the carriage returns and both files hash to their manifest entry exactly.
#
# `-text` disables eol translation in both directions, so what is checked out is
# what was committed, on every platform. The .wasm files survive today only by
# git's NUL-byte heuristic; a file the app verifies by hash should not depend on
# a heuristic, so they are marked explicitly.
public/assets/heart/** -text
public/assets/groloo-core/** -text
*.wasm binary

# Third-party bundle, shipped byte-for-byte and never edited here.
public/assets/hls.min.js -text
